Use QGuiApplication instead of QApplication.

QApplication comes from QtWidgets, which is not on Harbour's
allowed-libraries list: a Silica app is expected to use QtGui's
QGuiApplication, and Jolla's validator rejects a package whose binary
links libQt5Widgets.so.5.

Nothing here needs QtWidgets. The application object is only ever used
for exec() and quit(), both of which QGuiApplication provides -- see
`self->app->exec()` and `self->app->quit()` below the patched lines.

Upstream carries this unconditionally, with no feature to switch it off,
on the released crate and on master. See docs/HARBOUR.md.

--- a/src/qtdeclarative.rs
+++ b/src/qtdeclarative.rs
@@ -28,7 +28,7 @@
     #include <memory>
     #include <QtQuick/QtQuick>
     #include <QtCore/QDebug>
-    #include <QtWidgets/QApplication>
+    #include <QtGui/QGuiApplication>
     #include <QtQml/QQmlComponent>
 
     struct SingleApplicationGuard {
@@ -47,12 +47,12 @@
     };
 
     struct QmlEngineHolder : SingleApplicationGuard {
-        std::unique_ptr<QApplication> app;
+        std::unique_ptr<QGuiApplication> app;
         std::unique_ptr<QQmlApplicationEngine> engine;
         std::unique_ptr<QQuickView> view;
 
         QmlEngineHolder(int &argc, char **argv)
-            : app(new QApplication(argc, argv))
+            : app(new QGuiApplication(argc, argv))
             , engine(new QQmlApplicationEngine())
         {}
     };

Keep the sub-second part of a single_shot interval.

`interval.subsec_nanos() * 1e-6 as u32` parses as
`subsec_nanos() * (1e-6 as u32)`, and `1e-6 as u32` is 0, so every
interval lost its fraction: 1.5 s fired after 1 s, 200 ms at once.
`as_millis()` keeps it. QTimer takes an int, so intervals past
i32::MAX ms saturate rather than wrap to a negative one Qt refuses.

Upstream master still carries the same line.

--- a/src/lib.rs
+++ b/src/lib.rs
@@ -873,7 +873,8 @@
     let func_box: Box<dyn FnMut()> = Box::new(func);
     let mut func_raw = Box::into_raw(func_box);
 
-    let interval_ms: u32 = interval.as_secs() as u32 * 1000 + interval.subsec_nanos() * 1e-6 as u32;
+    // QTimer takes int milliseconds: saturate instead of wrapping.
+    let interval_ms = interval.as_millis().min(i32::MAX as u128) as i32;
 
     cpp!(unsafe [interval_ms as "int", mut func_raw as "FnBoxWrapper"] {
         QTimer::singleShot(interval_ms, std::move(func_raw));
